    "product_id": "30ba2eef-4cb9-4ee9-882f-276004501594",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 021124\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n324 AM PST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Flood-related impacts remain possible along the Big Sandy River in \n  southern Mohave County from rainfall runoff.\n\n* Temperatures will hover within a few degrees of seasonal normals\n  through the week with otherwise generally quiet conditions.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Sunday.\n\nMoisture will continue to wane across the forecast area with mostly \nsunny skies expected this afternoon. Following yesterday's rainfall, \nit is possible that water remains across low lying areas across \nLincoln, northeastern Clark, and Mohave counties, so will allow the \nFlood Watch to continue until its expiration time at 4 AM PST / 5 AM \nMST. Additionally, a Flood Warning remains in effect for the Big \nSandy River from Interstate 40 down to the Mohave / La Paz County \nline due to near-record flows as a result of runoff from last \nnight's rain. For context, Burro Creek at Highway 93, which flows \ninto the Big Sandy River, crested at 17.93 feet this afternoon, \nbreaking the previous record of 17.84 feet measured on February 8th, \n1993. As of 2 AM MST, Burro Creek at Highway 93 has subsided to \naround 12 feet. Will continue to assess the need for this Flood \nWarning through the morning. Cannot rule out patchy fog in low-lying \nareas over the next couple of mornings.\n\nA shortwave ridge will set up across the Desert Southwest for the \nfirst half of the work week, followed by zonal-to-northwesterly flow \naloft. Several shortwave troughs will ride along this flow through \nthe remainder of the work week, which will help to keep temperatures \nnear-seasonal with afternoon breeziness. On Friday and Saturday, a \nshortwave will pinch off of the upper-level flow and will attempt to \nclose off in the southeastern Pacific Ocean. The resultant north-\nsouth pressure gradients aross the forecast area will combine with \ntopographic enhancement along the Colorado River Valley to bring \ngusty north winds between 25 and 35 mph around Laughlin, Bullhead \nCity, and Needles Friday and Saturday afternoons. Expect increased \nwave heights on Lake Mohave as a result. \n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Breezy \nnorth-northwesterly winds with sustained speeds between 8 and 12 kts \nwill sporadically continue overnight; however, when speeds are \nlight, the direction will favor the southwest. Northwesterly winds \nwill pick up mid-morning with gradual veering through the day and \noccasional gusts to 15 kts. Expect directional variability between \n330 and 040 through the late-morning and afternoon hours. Winds will \nfall back into typical diurnal trends through the remainder of the \nTAF period after sunset today. FEW-SCT aoa 20 kft will return late-\nafternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Breezy northerly winds \nwith directional variability between 330 and 040 with occasional \ngusts to 15 kts expected for the Las Vegas Valley TAF sites. Winds \nwill be light and diurnal at KBIH. Westerly winds between 8 and 12 \nkts will continue to decrease at KDAG through the morning before \nbecoming light and diurnal through the remainder of the TAF period. \nGusty north winds will impact Colorado River Valley TAF sites this \nafternoon with gust speeds peaking between 15 and 20 kts. FEW-SCT \naoa 20 kft will push back into the region from west-to-east.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
